    "title": "Why we preregister our EHR analyses",
    "excerpt": "Writing the analysis plan before touching outcomes changes which questions we can answer honestly.",
    "content": {
      "html": "<p>Electronic health records invite endless re-analysis. Every extraction can be filtered, linked, and re-coded, and each choice moves the estimate a little. We now <strong>preregister</strong> every EHR analysis before we look at outcome data.</p><h2>What goes in the plan</h2><ul><li><p>The target trial: eligibility, treatment strategies, and time zero.</p></li><li><p>The causal diagram and the adjustment set it implies.</p></li><li><p>How we handle <em>missing</em> covariates and loss to follow-up.</p></li></ul><figure><img src=\"{{ASSETS}}/9101/conversions/dag-confounding-large.svg\" alt=\"A directed acyclic graph with treatment, outcome, and two confounders\" data-media-id=\"101\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The causal diagram we commit to before looking at outcomes.</figcaption></figure><h2>What it costs</h2><p>About two weeks per project. The plan template is on <a href=\"https://osf.io/\">OSF</a>, and the protocol for our registry linkage study is linked from the <a href=\"/projects/registry-linkage\">project page</a>.</p><blockquote><p>A plan written after the results is a description of the results.</p></blockquote>",
