    "title": "Hierarchical models for small-area rates",
    "excerpt": "Why raw county rates mislead and how partial pooling helps.",
    "content": {
      "html": "<p>Raw rates from small counties swing wildly. A county with 40 children and 3 cases has a rate of 75 per 1,000, and nobody believes it.</p><figure><img src=\"{{ASSETS}}/9102/conversions/county-rates-map-large.svg\" alt=\"Map of smoothed county asthma rates in shades of blue\" data-media-id=\"102\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Smoothed rates shrink small counties toward their neighbors.</figcaption></figure><h2>Partial pooling</h2><p>A hierarchical model shrinks each county toward the regional mean in proportion to how little data it has. In code the filter is <code>rate &lt; 0.05 &amp; n &gt; 30</code> for counties we trust without pooling.</p><pre><code class=\"language-stan\">model {\n  theta ~ normal(mu, tau);\n  y ~ binomial_logit(n, theta);\n}</code></pre>",
